      "| **GE AI** is a set of data‑science, machine‑learning, and analytics tools that GE Digital (the industrial‑technology arm of GE) offers to its customers. | • Sensors on industrial assets generate terabytes of “machine‑state” data. <br>• GE’s AI platform ingests that data, applies statistical models, deep‑learning networks, and rule‑based logic. <br>• The platform produces real‑time insights, predictive maintenance alerts, and optimization recommendations. | • **Predictive maintenance** – forecast when a turbine blade or pump will fail before a catastrophic breakdown. <br>• **Process optimization** – automatically tune combustion engines or chemical reactors for maximum efficiency. <br>• **Quality control** – detect defects in manufacturing lines in real time. <br>• **Health‑tech** – analyze imaging or wearables for early disease detection (e.g., GE’s partnership with IBM Watson). | • Cuts unplanned downtime (often 20‑30 % savings). <br>• Extends asset life and safety margins. <br>• Enables “digital twins” that simulate plant behavior under different scenarios. <br>• Drives a new revenue stream for GE Digital and its OEM partners. |\n",
      "\n",
      "### Key components\n",
      "\n",
      "1. **Predix AI** – GE’s industrial‑IoT platform that hosts data pipelines, edge‑computing nodes, and the AI models.\n",
      "2. **Edge analytics** – lightweight inference engines that run directly on equipment to provide instant alerts.\n",
      "3. **Cloud‑based model training** – high‑performance GPU clusters that train models on historical data from thousands of machines.\n",
      "4. **Industry‑specific AI libraries** – pre‑built models for aviation, power, oil & gas, healthcare, and manufacturing.\n",
      "\n",
